---
import ToolLayout from '../layouts/ToolLayout.astro';
import AdSlot from '../components/AdSlot.astro';
import ExtractImages from '../islands/ExtractImages';

const faqs = [
  { q: 'Are images extracted at full resolution?', a: 'Yes. Each picture is saved at the resolution stored in the PDF, not the size it appears on the page. Photos are saved as high-quality JPG and images with transparency as PNG.' },
  { q: 'Why were no images found?', a: 'The PDF may contain only text and vector drawings, which aren’t images. To save whole pages as pictures, use <a href="/pdf-to-jpg">PDF to JPG</a>.' },
  { q: 'Why is a scanned PDF one image per page?', a: 'A scan stores each page as one big picture, so that’s exactly what gets extracted.' },
  { q: 'Can I extract images from a protected PDF?', a: 'Only after unlocking it with its password using <a href="/unlock-pdf">Unlock PDF</a>.' },
  { q: 'Is my PDF uploaded?', a: 'No. Images are read by pdf.js inside your browser. The PDF never leaves your device.' },
];
---
<ToolLayout
  slug="extract-images-from-pdf"
  title="Extract Images from PDF – Save All Pictures Free | PDFSherpa"
  description="Save every photo and picture inside a PDF at full resolution, as JPG or PNG, one by one or as a zip. Free, fast and private: no upload."
  h1="Extract Images from PDF"
  lead="Pull every photo and picture out of a PDF at its original resolution, then save them one by one or all at once as a zip. Nothing is uploaded."
  howToTitle="How to Extract Images from a PDF"
  steps={['Select or drop your PDF.', 'Wait a moment while every page is scanned for images.', 'Save single images or <strong>Download all</strong> as a .zip.']}
  faqs={faqs}
  related={['pdf-to-jpg', 'compress-image', 'image-converter', 'pdf-to-text']}
>
  <ExtractImages client:load />

  <Fragment slot="content">
    <h2>What this tool does</h2>
    <p>
      PDFs often contain the original photos you need: product shots in a brochure, figures in a report, pictures in an e-book. Instead of taking
      screenshots, Extract Images saves each embedded picture as its own file, at the resolution stored in the PDF.
    </p>

    <h2>Step by step</h2>
    <ol>
      <li><strong>Open your PDF.</strong> Every page is scanned automatically, with progress shown.</li>
      <li><strong>Review the gallery.</strong> Each image shows its page number and pixel size. Duplicates, such as a logo repeated on every page, are saved once.</li>
      <li><strong>Save</strong> individual images or download them all as a zip.</li>
    </ol>

    <AdSlot variant="inline" slot="tool-article" />

    <h2>Good to know</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Tiny graphics under 24 pixels, such as icons and masks, are skipped to keep the result useful.</li>
      <li>Images with transparency are saved as PNG. Everything else is saved as high-quality JPG.</li>
      <li>Charts and diagrams drawn as vector graphics aren’t images. Capture them with <a href="/pdf-to-jpg">PDF to JPG</a> instead.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Examples</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Product catalogue:</strong> save every product photo from a supplier’s PDF to use on your online shop, with permission.</li>
      <li><strong>Scanned document:</strong> get each scanned page as a separate JPG to upload to a portal that only accepts images.</li>
      <li><strong>Reports and theses:</strong> recover your own figures and photos when you’ve lost the originals.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Respect copyright: only reuse images you own or have permission to use.</p>

    <h2>Limitations</h2>
    <p>
      Some special image types, such as certain CMYK printing images, may look slightly different from the PDF because colours are converted for screens.
      Very large PDFs with hundreds of photos need plenty of memory, so on phones, split them first with <a href="/split-pdf">Split PDF</a>.
    </p>

    <h2>Privacy</h2>
    <p>Everything happens inside your browser with Mozilla’s pdf.js. Your PDF and its images are never uploaded.</p>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
